# Trivy ignore file for cowork's customer-facing Docker image.
#
# Each entry is a HIGH/CRITICAL CVE that we have triaged and accepted
# as residual risk. Every entry MUST cite:
#   1. The package and current Red Hat UBI 9 version.
#   2. Why no fix is applied (most commonly: no upstream patch yet).
#   3. Why the residual risk is acceptable in our threat model.
#
# This file is checked into the repo so the customer (and any auditor)
# can see exactly what we've ignored and why. Re-review on every base
# image bump — `docker pull registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9-minimal`
# then re-scan and remove any entry whose CVE is now patched in the
# new base.
#
# Last reviewed: 2026-07-01 against
#   registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9-minimal@sha256:
#     5b74fce9d6e629942a0c6dc0f546c193e70d7f974d999a48c948c53dd3d36362
# (Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9.8), with the cowork-server 0.26.6.26.1
# Python closure installed into /opt/venv on python3.12.
#
# Current posture: with the two no-upstream-fix suppressions below
# (libacl/libattr), Trivy reports 0 HIGH / 0 CRITICAL on both
# linux/amd64 and linux/arm64 across the OS layer and the entire Python
# dependency closure. Everything with an available fix is applied at
# build time: `microdnf update` pulls current Red Hat errata (e.g.
# openssl-libs 3.5.5-4.el9_8 for CVE-2026-45447) and the Dockerfile
# pins a PyJWT >= 2.13.0 floor (CVE-2026-48526).
#
# Note: the 9.8 base errata fixed the krb5-libs, libcap, and python
# HIGHs that the previous 9.7 digest carried. The four gnutls HIGHs that
# still have no upstream fix (CVE-2026-33845/-33846/-42009/-42010) are no
# longer suppressed here — the runtime stage REMOVES the entire unused
# dnf/glib2/gnupg/gnutls package closure (see Dockerfile "Final
# hardening" step), so those packages don't exist in the shipped image
# and never reach a scan.
#
# Threat model summary: cowork is a FastAPI HTTP server that listens on
# a single loopback port (default 26866). It does NOT terminate TLS, does
# NOT speak DTLS / WebRTC, does NOT speak Kerberos, does NOT parse
# untrusted XML on its HTTP API (the API is JSON-only), and does NOT load
# arbitrary terminfo from network input.

# expat — Expat XML parser, linked by CPython's pyexpat and therefore a
# hard dependency of the python3.12 interpreter (cannot be removed
# without removing the interpreter). CVE-2026-45186 is a DoS via crafted
# XML input. As of the 2026-06-09 review the UBI 9.8 base ships an expat
# that neither Trivy nor ECR flags for this CVE, so it does NOT currently
# appear in a scan of the shipped image — this entry is retained purely
# as defense-in-depth, in case a scanner with a different advisory DB
# (e.g. the customer's Snyk) still reports it. Even if surfaced, the risk
# is acceptable: cowork's HTTP API consumes JSON only and exposes no
# untrusted-XML parsing path, so the crafted-input vector is unreachable
# from the network surface; DoS-class (no RCE). Remove this entry once
# confirmed permanently fixed across the scanners we care about.
CVE-2026-45186

# libacl / libattr — POSIX ACL and extended-attribute libraries. No
# upstream fix available as of the 2026-07-01 review (status: affected,
# no errata). Cannot be removed: libacl.so.1 is a hard runtime
# dependency of coreutils-single, sed, libarchive, rpm-libs and
# shadow-utils in the UBI 9 minimal base. Both CVEs are LOCAL symlink-
# traversal privilege escalations exercised through the ACL/xattr
# *utilities* (getfattr/setfacl-style tools operating on attacker-
# controlled paths). The `acl` and `attr` tool packages are NOT
# installed in this image, the container runs as a single non-root user
# (uid 1000) with no other local principals, and nothing in cowork's
# runtime invokes ACL/xattr operations on untrusted paths — the attack
# preconditions (local multi-user filesystem race) do not exist here.
# Re-review on every base bump; remove once Red Hat ships errata.
CVE-2026-54369
CVE-2026-54371
